Cornelius had been struggling for years with serious health issues. In 1982, he underwent a 21-hour brain surgery to correct a congenital abnormality in blood vessels of his brain. After this procedure, he continued to suffer from persistent health problems, including epileptic seizures, which worsened during the last six months of his life. This physical decline led to a deep depression. According to the autopsy report, he called his son early in the morning on the day of his death and said, “I don’t know how much longer I can take this.”

Personal Struggles
In addition to his health problems, Cornelius faced personal setbacks. In 2008, he was arrested for domestic violence against his then-wife, which led to a conviction and three years’ probation. During the divorce proceedings in 2009, he stated that he was suffering from "significant health issues" and expressed a desire to finalize the divorce before he died.

A Reclusive Life
According to his son, Tony Cornelius, Don was a very private person who often kept his feelings to himself. Although his family knew he was struggling, they didn’t realize how deep his depression really was. 

Cultural Legacy
Through Soul Train he provided a national platform for African American artists. His iconic sign-off at the end of each episode—“Love, peace, and soul”—remains a powerful reflection of his impact on music and television history.